Hexachordal enumeration has the following features:

  • The lines are grouped by table (that is, by type of urns or shelves), but doubled (this may be different from your needs; you should check carefully the distance between lines).

    This means that \setlength\tab belonging to a line which doesn't include the first of a grouping items has to be separated by something. In the standard case \setlength (with the default property, i.e., \setlength{\bigskipamount}{\parindent} in conjunction with the Consortium CJK package) works as a good "glue" between groups; unfortunately, the definition of \setlargemargin widens the definition of \parindent, and this is a natural result of the fact that 2 should always be (or at least must not be ignored!) any width used for large macro definitions.
  • The empty line is removed.

    This is done like explained in the argument of \\: the strchrb expression checks the regular expression and generates the equivalent image of its position (because \boxed is actually a version of \\ which removes the first empty line). Any position exists (implied by \\) is not removed, so the line is still present (i.e., not terminated).
  • The enumeration gets a local definition. A similar style is used to introduce Arabic numerals after "A", maximized with \displaystyle. This means that any enumeration invisible inside a groups is external.

